  "passage": "Lena's watch stopped at 3:17. She planned to throw it away, but her brother said it looked nice and asked to keep it.\nHe wore it even though it did not tell time. His friends asked why.\nHe said, \"It reminds me that time is not only numbers.\"\nLena thought this was silly until she learned that 3:17 was the time their family had arrived at their new home years ago. Their mother had written it in an old diary.\nThe stopped watch became meaningful by accident.\nIt no longer measured passing time, but it marked one important moment. Sometimes an object stops working as a tool and starts working as a memory.",
